  "translation": {
    "literal": "I left home when I was young and I return when I am old; my country accent is unchanged, but the hair at my temples has thinned. The children I meet do not know who I am; they smile and ask, \"Where have you come from, visitor?\"",
    "poetic": "I left home a boy and I come back an old man. My accent is the same, though the hair at my temples is thin. The children I meet look at me as a stranger, and ask with a smile where the visitor has come from."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "poet",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "A return after fifty years",
      "body": "He Zhizhang (659–744) left his home in Yongxing (near modern Hangzhou) to serve at court and came back when he retired, in his eighties. This is the first of two poems he wrote called 回鄉偶書 (\"written by chance on returning home\")."
    },
    {
      "id": "contrast",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "What changed and what did not",
      "body": "The poem is built on contrasts. The first line is about time, the second line is about the poet (voice the same, hair thin), the third is about the children (they do not know him) and the fourth is the children's smiling question. That last question is both polite and heartbreaking: in his own village, a man who still speaks like a local is asked where he is from."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 回 · 衰 · 來",
      "body": "Lines one, two and four end in 回 (huí), 衰 (cuī) and 來 (lái), in the 灰 rhyme. 衰 is normally shuāi, but is read cuī here to keep the rhyme, as taught in PRC schools."
    },
    {
      "id": "readings",
      "kind": "other",
      "title": "Words with more than one reading",
      "body": "少 is read shào in 少小 (\"young\"), not shǎo. 處 in 何處 is read chù. 衰 is cuī, not shuāi, in this poem."
    }
  ],
